# =============================================================================
# Build context — ALLOWLIST, not a blocklist.
#
# Deliberately inverted compared to the sibling services, whose .dockerignore
# opens with `*.json`, `*.png`, `*.txt`. Copied here as-is, those three lines
# would silently drop `document_processing/models/*/ONNX/model.json` — the input
# shapes, class lists and pre/post-processing steps for every model. The image
# would build, push and start, and die on the first `Pipeline()`.
#
# So: exclude everything, then name what the image needs. A new dependency fails
# loudly at build time instead of being quietly omitted, and the Dockerfile
# asserts the model count as a second line of defence.
# =============================================================================
*

# The library and the service that wraps it.
!document_processing/
!service/

# Anonymised documents. Needed at runtime, not just for tests: the startup
# warmup uses one, and the pre-computed seed manifest references them by path.
# Only ever these — never a real user document (see CLAUDE.md).
!samples/

# Frontend sources for the build stage. Its own artefacts are re-excluded below.
!web/

!requirements2026.txt
!requirements-service.txt

# The ports. This file predates them, and the allowlist is exactly as strict as
# advertised: the first `docker build` of the Go image failed with
# `"/ports/go": not found` — the context simply had no such directory. Loud, at
# build time, which is what the inverted list is for.
!ports/

# ---- Re-excluded from the allowlist above ----------------------------------
web/node_modules
web/dist
**/__pycache__
**/*.pyc
**/.pytest_cache
service/seed_data/**/*.tmp.*

# HOST build output under ports/ must never enter the context. bin/ holds a
# 19 MB Windows binary plus the four MSYS2 DLLs that D-09 requires beside it —
# useless to a Linux image, and shipping a stale host binary into a container is
# a genuinely confusing failure. The image builds its own from source.
ports/*/bin/
ports/**/*.exe
ports/**/*.dll
ports/**/*.so

# .NET build output, and `**/` is LOAD-BEARING: a .dockerignore pattern is matched
# against the whole path, so a bare `obj/` matches only a top-level directory and
# every ports/dotnet/src/*/obj/ would sail straight into the context.
#
# That is not hypothetical — it cost the first .NET build. The host's Windows
# project.assets.json landed on top of the container's freshly restored one, and
# `dotnet publish` then failed with "Package OpenCvSharp4 was not found. It might
# have been deleted since NuGet restore", because the assets file it was handed
# pointed at C:\Users\...\.nuget and at the WINDOWS native runtime package. A
# message about a missing package, whose actual cause was packaging.
#
# obj/ also records the package feed URL, which is a second reason it must not
# travel: this repository is public and the host's feed is not.
# JVM build output, and `**/` is LOAD-BEARING for exactly the reason the .NET rules
# below spell out: a bare `build/` matches only a top-level directory, so every
# ports/java/*/build/ would travel into the context — 657 MB of fat jar per module,
# and a Gradle build directory carrying absolute HOST paths that the container build
# would then resolve against nothing.
#
# .gradle/ is the daemon's own state, including the local file-dependency path to
# RDOCS_OPENCV_HOME, which is a Windows path. .kotlin/ is the compiler's session
# directory. Neither means anything inside an image.
ports/java/**/build/
ports/java/**/.gradle/
ports/java/**/.kotlin/
ports/java/.gradle/
ports/java/.kotlin/

ports/dotnet/**/bin/
ports/dotnet/**/obj/
**/project.assets.json
**/*.nuget.g.props
**/*.nuget.g.targets
